  on_the_salt: {
    "id": "on_the_salt",
    "name": "On the Salt",
    "type": "data_log",
    "body": "ON THE SALT\n— a pilgrim's account, copied by hand\n\nThe derricks come first. Long before any wall or minaret, you see them: black bones standing in a white sea, leaning the way the wind has asked them to lean for two centuries.\n\nThey tell you the Chukai is a death zone. They are not wrong. They are only incomplete.\n\nWalk it at dawn. The salt takes the light before the sky does, so for a few minutes the ground is brighter than the air above it, and you understand — not as doctrine, as a thing your eyes have simply told you — why the first scholars called this a scar and not a wound. A wound is what is still happening. A scar is what you are permitted to build on.\n\nIt was here, in a basin the war fused to glass, that Syed the First found the Library.\n\nI have no gift for argument and I will not try to make one. I will only set down what I know: that the emptiest place on this Peninsula gave us every living thing we have, and I do not know what to do with a fact of that shape except keep walking through it, and write it down, and hope the writing counts as thanks.",
    "category_path": [
      "Found Texts",
      "The Peninsula"
    ]
  },
  peribahasa_baru: {
    "id": "peribahasa_baru",
    "name": "Peribahasa Baru",
    "type": "data_log",
    "body": "PERIBAHASA BARU\nProverbs of the new country — collected by a schoolteacher who no longer has a school\n\n*Air mahal, darah murah.*\nWater is dear, blood is cheap. — Said of any argument that begins at a well.\n\n*Bagai ghoul menunggu cermin.*\nLike a ghoul waiting for a mirror. — Of a person hoping for news that will not come, or will not be kind when it does.\n\n*Jangan percaya jalan yang terlalu bersih.*\nNever trust a road that is too clean. — Someone is keeping it clear. Ask why before you walk it.\n\n*Syarikat sudah mati, borang masih hidup.*\nThe company is dead, the form is still alive. — Of Federation paperwork, and of anything else that outlives its purpose.\n\n*Tanam padi, tuai borang.*\nPlant rice, harvest paperwork.\n\n*Dua besar, satu meja.*\nTwo big men, one table. — Of a council that must agree. Said quietly, and not in New Town.\n\nCollector's note: the old proverbs were about rice, rain and patience. The new ones are about water, paperwork, and people who will not say plainly what they want. I do not think this is an improvement.\n\nI write them down anyway. A language that has stopped making these has stopped being used.",
    "category_path": [
      "Found Texts",
      "The Peninsula"
    ]
  },
  the_city_of_the_dead: {
    "id": "the_city_of_the_dead",
    "name": "The City of the Dead",
    "type": "data_log",
    "body": "WHAT YOU SHOULD KNOW BEFORE YOU GO IN\n\nThey still write it Baru on the old signs. Nobody has called it new in two hundred years.\n\nOne. The husks are not empty. Assume every floor above the third is occupied and you will be right often enough to come home.\n\nTwo. There are two kinds in there and at distance you will not tell them apart. The ones who remember will talk to you — will want to talk to you, will follow you a long way for the chance. The ones who don't will not stop and will not tire and do not need to see you to find you.\n\nThree. Some of the monorail hubs still hold power. This is not the gift it sounds like. Light draws the second kind the way a well draws everything else.\n\nFour. The ministries are gone. Their doors are not. Several still lock, and several still lock behind you, and the Federation that cut those keys does not exist in any form that would come and let you out.\n\nFive. Take twice the water you think you need. There is nothing to drink in the City of the Dead that has not been dead as long as the city.",
    "category_path": [
      "Found Texts",
      "The Peninsula"
    ]
  },
